course: dictionary
lesson: 13316
title: Gripping: exciting or holding tight
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. «Gripping» is one of them. Use the clues around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: «The last chapter was gripping, and I read it under the covers with a torch.» A gripping story holds your attention so strongly that you cannot stop reading. The clue is reading under the covers with a torch, so gripping means exciting here.
explain: «Sam was gripping the rope tightly as he climbed.» Here gripping comes from the verb grip, which means to hold something tightly. The clue is the rope and the climbing. Try the swap test: «holding the rope tightly» still makes sense, so this is the holding meaning.
fact: Gripping can be an adjective: it describes a book, film or story that is very exciting.
fact: «A gripping story» means a story that holds your attention so you cannot stop.
fact: Gripping is also the -ing form of the verb «grip»: grip, grips, gripped, gripping.
fact: «Gripping the handlebars» means holding the handlebars tightly.
fact: When you climb or use equipment, grip it firmly and hold on tight to stay safe.
exercise: «The film was so gripping that nobody in the room moved.» What does gripping mean? | Very cold | Slow and boring | *Very exciting, so you cannot look away | Holding something tightly
exercise: «Jade was gripping the rail as the boat rocked.» What is Jade doing? | *Holding the rail tightly | Painting the rail | Reading a book about the rail | Lying down on the rail
exercise: Which sentence uses «gripping» to mean holding tightly? | The gripping ending was a surprise. | *The monkey was gripping the branch with both hands. | The gripping film was two hours long. | The gripping story kept me awake.
test: «The detective story was so gripping that Ella finished it in one night.» What does gripping mean here? | Holding something tightly | *Exciting, so you cannot stop reading | Boring and very long | Wet and muddy
test: «Ben kept gripping the handlebars on the steep hill.» What is Ben doing? | Reading a gripping book | Waving to his friends | Laughing at a joke | *Holding the handlebars tightly
exam: Which word fills the gap? «The mystery was so ___ that I read until midnight.» | gravel | *gripping | gentle | grumpy
